# AN ALPINE MICROVM WITH NO DESKTOP: A ROOT FILESYSTEM, A SERIAL LOGIN, NO PCIe.
#
# THIS IS THE FIRST OF THE THREE HEADLESS MICROVMS - Alpine, Debian, NixOS - and it is the one that
# works without anything being arranged. Alpine's kernel is built with the option the other two
# disagree about:
#
#	Alpine 3.21   CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=y   CMDLINE_DEVICES=y
#	Debian 6.12   CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=m   # CMDLINE_DEVICES is not set
#
# so qemu's own command-line description of the MMIO disk is enough here, and the guest finds
# /dev/vda with no help. The Debian twin of this file needs `acpi=on` for the same result; both are
# stated on the machine type by the engine, so neither file has to know.
#
# TRAILING `without a display` IS NOT DECORATION. The three shapes are `with a desktop` (a
# virtio-gpu adapter that X can draw on), `with a console` (a text-mode ISA VGA adapter), and
# `without a display` (no adapter at all, serial only). A headless microvm that asked for a console
# would get an adapter nothing ever writes to, and its recording would be a black field instead of a
# stated absence.
#
# NOTHING HERE INSTALLS ALPINE. A microvm has no firmware to read a boot sector, so qemu is handed
# the kernel and the initramfs directly and the disk arrives holding a system -
# `.github/alpine-rootfs.sh` builds the three files.
#
# THIS FILE REPLACES A DISKLESS ONE. The earlier `verify.walk` booted Alpine's netboot kernel and
# initramfs with the ISO attached as a second disk, and asserted `login` on the console - which it
# reached, because Alpine's netboot guest runs entirely from tmpfs and a `modloop` squashfs. That
# proved the vocabulary launched a guest. It did NOT prove a machine with a DISK, and the difference
# was measured the moment a root filesystem was attached: the stock netboot initramfs carries 72
# modules and ext4 is not one of them, so the guest answers `mount: /dev/vda: No such device` and
# drops to an emergency shell. This one boots the disk it was given.
walkthrough "alpine microvm - a root filesystem, a serial login, and no PCIe"
resolution 1024x768
# THE VIDEO IS AN ARTIFACT EVEN WHEN THE GUEST DRAWS NOTHING, so its size is stated.
recording_resolution 1024x768
# THE THREE FILES THE CI STEP BUILDS, named here so the boot step can refer to them.
step "needs alpine-vmlinuz alpine-initramfs alpine.qcow2"
  needs [alpine-vmlinuz, alpine-initramfs, alpine.qcow2]
# THE ROOT FILESYSTEM, BUILT BY THE CI STEP AND NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE.


step "disk alpine.qcow2"
  disk alpine.qcow2
# `console=ttyS0` IS THE ONLY PLACE THIS GUEST CAN BE READ, because it has no adapter. `tty0` is
# deliberately absent: asking the kernel to mirror to a console that does not exist is noise in
# the log this file asserts on.


step "boot as a microvm from kernel \"alpine-vmlinuz\" and initramfs \"alpine-initramfs\" with \"console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4\" without a display with memory 768, waiting up to 180s"
  boot
    machine "microvm,accel=kvm,acpi=on,pic=on,rtc=on,pcie=off,graphics=off"
    args [["-m", "768"], ["-chardev", "socket,id=qga0,path={{work}}/qga.sock,server=on,wait=off"], ["-device", "isa-serial,chardev=qga0"], ["-drive", "file={{disk}},if=none,format=qcow2,id=target{{discard}}"], ["-device", "virtio-blk-device,drive=target"], ["-netdev", "tap,id=net0,ifname=qemutap0,script=no,downscript=no"], ["-device", "virtio-net-device,netdev=net0,mac=52:54:00:56:4d:01"], ["-kernel", "{{work}}/alpine-vmlinuz"], ["-initrd", "{{work}}/alpine-initramfs"], ["-append", "console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4"]]
    timeout 180
# THE FIRST FACT: the kernel found the disk. An MMIO disk that is not claimed leaves the guest at
# `Begin: Running /scripts/local-block ... done.` until it gives up, so this line is the
# difference between a boot and a wait.


step "the console will say \"vda\""
  until vda
  from console
  timeout 180
# AND THE SECOND: userspace finished and A SHELL IS WAITING, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS `login:`.
#
# THE ROOT FILESYSTEM AUTOLOGS vmuser. `inittab` runs
#
#	ttyS0::respawn:/sbin/getty -L -n -l /sbin/autologin ttyS0 115200 vt100
#
# so there is never a `login:` prompt to wait for: the program getty starts is `/sbin/autologin`,
# which execs `su - vmuser` and prints the user's own prompt. MEASURED on the console:
#
#	udhcpc: lease of 172.30.0.2 obtained from 172.30.0.1
#	 * Starting sshd ... [ ok ]
#	 * Starting local ... [ ok ]
#	localhost:~$
#
# AND THE AUTOLOGIN IS NOT A CONVENIENCE. PipeWire refuses root (`ConditionUser=!root`), so a guest
# whose console is root cannot start a Wayland session - which is what the arch microvm desktops
# need. The console belongs to vmuser for the same reason the desktop does.
#
# THIS FILE ASSERTED `login` UNTIL A CI RUN SHOWED THE PROMPT WAS `localhost:~$` - the assertion was
# written against a getty that never worked (its `-a` flag is util-linux's, not busybox's), so the
# sentence about `login:` described a screen that could not exist.


step "the console will say \"localhost:~$\""
  until localhost:~$
  from console
  timeout 180
# TWENTY SECONDS OF A GUEST AT A PROMPT, so the recording is a machine that stayed up rather than
# a boot log that scrolled past.


step "wait 20s"
  settle 20


step "stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s"
  stop kill
  timeout 60
